1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,201
You've now got every piece: a brief system, a generation pipeline, a pre-flight

2
00:00:05,201 --> 00:00:10,114
checklist, a testing architecture, an honest read, a weekly sprint, and a pitch.

3
00:00:10,114 --> 00:00:15,211
That's a lot to hold at once, and trying to stand up all of it in the first week is

4
00:00:15,211 --> 00:00:18,957
how a genuinely good system collapses under its own ambition.

5
00:00:18,957 --> 00:00:22,150
This lecture sequences it into three phases instead.

6
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:27,001
Not because the pieces have to happen in strict isolation, but because each one

7
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,765
needs the last one in place to actually work.

8
00:00:29,765 --> 00:00:34,616
A testing architecture built before a cleared batch exists has nothing to test.

9
00:00:34,616 --> 00:00:39,037
A pitch built before a single honest report exists has nothing to prove.

10
00:00:39,037 --> 00:00:42,722
Ninety days is a real target, not an arbitrary round number.

11
00:00:42,722 --> 00:00:47,880
It's roughly the time it takes to run several full sprint cycles, enough for Section

12
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:52,363
9.1's rhythm to become a genuine habit rather than something you're still

13
00:00:52,363 --> 00:00:54,574
consciously assembling week to week.

14
00:00:54,574 --> 00:00:59,425
Why three phases specifically, rather than a longer list of smaller milestones?

15
00:00:59,425 --> 00:01:04,154
Because each phase maps to a genuine dependency, not an arbitrary checkpoint.

16
00:01:04,154 --> 00:01:09,128
You can't run honestly before you've built properly, and you can't credibly prove

17
00:01:09,128 --> 00:01:11,523
anything before you've run it for real.

18
00:01:11,523 --> 00:01:16,681
Collapsing the three into one rushed month skips straight past the dependencies that

19
00:01:16,681 --> 00:01:19,137
make each phase actually mean something.

20
00:01:19,687 --> 00:01:23,743
Notice phase one has no scoring in it at all, and that's deliberate.

21
00:01:23,743 --> 00:01:28,454
Section 8's honest read needs real data to read, and real data needs a properly

22
00:01:28,454 --> 00:01:31,496
built, isolated testing architecture running first.

23
00:01:31,496 --> 00:01:36,147
Rushing into scoring before the structure exists just produces noise you can't

24
00:01:36,147 --> 00:01:37,042
actually trust.

25
00:01:37,042 --> 00:01:39,427
Phase two is where the real habit forms.

26
00:01:39,427 --> 00:01:42,529
One sprint cycle proves you can run the system once.

27
00:01:42,529 --> 00:01:47,121
Two or three prove you can run it as a rhythm, which is the entire difference

28
00:01:47,121 --> 00:01:49,447
Section 9 spent five lectures building.

29
00:01:49,447 --> 00:01:54,158
Don't skip to phase three until this phase has actually happened, not just been

30
00:01:54,158 --> 00:01:54,635
planned.

31
00:01:54,635 --> 00:01:59,287
Phase three only applies if you're taking this toward outside work or a bigger

32
00:01:59,287 --> 00:02:00,301
internal mandate.

33
00:02:00,301 --> 00:02:05,131
If you're running this purely for your own account, days sixty-one through ninety

34
00:02:05,131 --> 00:02:09,962
are simply more sprint cycles, the habit continuing to compound rather than a new

35
00:02:09,962 --> 00:02:10,856
phase starting.

36
00:02:10,856 --> 00:02:15,389
Adjust the exact day counts to your own reality without abandoning the order

37
00:02:15,389 --> 00:02:16,343
underneath them.

38
00:02:16,343 --> 00:02:21,293
A slower-moving account might need forty-five days to reach a genuinely clean first

39
00:02:21,293 --> 00:02:22,367
batch, not thirty.

40
00:02:22,367 --> 00:02:23,082
That's fine.

41
00:02:23,082 --> 00:02:27,913
What matters is that building still comes before running, and running still comes

42
00:02:27,913 --> 00:02:31,014
before proving, whatever the calendar actually says.

43
00:02:31,564 --> 00:02:36,136
Notice every line traces back to a specific section, not a vague intention.

44
00:02:36,136 --> 00:02:37,233
That's on purpose.

45
00:02:37,233 --> 00:02:42,354
A thirty-sixty-ninety plan built from generic goals drifts the moment real work gets

46
00:02:42,354 --> 00:02:42,659
busy.

47
00:02:42,659 --> 00:02:47,657
One built from this course's actual artifacts has a concrete definition of done at

48
00:02:47,657 --> 00:02:48,694
every checkpoint.

49
00:02:48,694 --> 00:02:51,985
If you're behind this schedule, that's genuinely fine.

50
00:02:51,985 --> 00:02:54,241
The order matters more than the pace.

51
00:02:54,241 --> 00:02:59,361
Someone who takes six months to reach day ninety, in this sequence, is in a stronger

52
00:02:59,361 --> 00:03:04,116
position than someone who rushed all three phases into three weeks and skipped

53
00:03:04,116 --> 00:03:07,652
straight to pitching without ever running an honest cycle.

54
00:03:07,652 --> 00:03:12,590
Keep this checklist somewhere you'll actually revisit it, the same advice Section

55
00:03:12,590 --> 00:03:14,540
9.4 already gave the swipe file.

56
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:18,320
A plan checked once and then forgotten is a plan in name only.

57
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:23,135
Checked weekly, even briefly, it does the actual job of telling you which phase

58
00:03:23,135 --> 00:03:28,256
you're honestly in right now, not which phase you assumed you'd be in by this point.

59
00:03:28,806 --> 00:03:33,819
One honest trap worth naming, because the pressure to show results early is real,

60
00:03:33,819 --> 00:03:37,160
whether it's coming from a client or your own manager.

61
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:42,297
Jumping to phase three before phase two has actually happened means pitching a case

62
00:03:42,297 --> 00:03:47,310
study that doesn't exist yet, or reporting a ratio you're guessing at rather than

63
00:03:47,310 --> 00:03:48,547
one you've measured.

64
00:03:48,547 --> 00:03:53,622
That's the same mistake Section 9.5 warned against in a report, just moved earlier

65
00:03:53,622 --> 00:03:54,612
in the timeline.

66
00:03:54,612 --> 00:03:59,810
It doesn't stop being dishonest because the pressure came before the work instead of

67
00:03:59,810 --> 00:04:00,367
after it.

68
00:04:00,367 --> 00:04:05,256
If someone's asking for results before day sixty, tell them honestly what phase

69
00:04:05,256 --> 00:04:06,432
you're actually in.

70
00:04:06,432 --> 00:04:11,321
"Building the testing architecture, first real read expected in two weeks" is a

71
00:04:11,321 --> 00:04:12,682
true, useful sentence.

72
00:04:12,682 --> 00:04:14,786
A fabricated early win is neither.

73
00:04:14,786 --> 00:04:18,623
Would that honest sentence actually satisfy the person asking?

74
00:04:18,623 --> 00:04:20,789
Often, yes, more than you'd expect.

75
00:04:20,789 --> 00:04:25,740
Most reasonable stakeholders can handle "here's exactly where we are" far better

76
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:30,629
than they can handle discovering later that an early result was inflated to buy

77
00:04:30,629 --> 00:04:30,938
time.

78
00:04:30,938 --> 00:04:34,094
The honest answer costs a moment of discomfort now.

79
00:04:34,094 --> 00:04:37,189
The fabricated one costs trust later, all at once.

80
00:04:37,739 --> 00:04:42,588
Section eleven is the capstone: one full product, one full sprint, brief to honest

81
00:04:42,588 --> 00:04:47,378
read, exactly as this plan's first sixty days describes, compressed into a guided

82
00:04:47,378 --> 00:04:50,749
exercise you can complete before starting the real thing.

83
00:04:50,749 --> 00:04:52,997
Before that, pick your actual day one.

84
00:04:52,997 --> 00:04:57,905
Not a hypothetical future Monday, the specific date you'll open Section 2's product

85
00:04:57,905 --> 00:05:01,217
truth doc for whatever you're actually building this on.

86
00:05:01,217 --> 00:05:06,126
A plan without a start date is a plan that starts whenever motivation happens to be

87
00:05:06,126 --> 00:05:09,674
highest, which is a schedule that rarely arrives on its own.

88
00:05:09,674 --> 00:05:14,464
Write that date down somewhere visible, the same discipline this whole course has

89
00:05:14,464 --> 00:05:17,067
asked of every other decision worth keeping.

90
00:05:17,067 --> 00:05:21,206
A start date that only exists in your head is one more good intention.

91
00:05:21,206 --> 00:05:26,174
A start date written down, next to the checklist from this lecture, is the beginning

92
00:05:26,174 --> 00:05:27,653
of an actual ninety days.
